Subject: [PATCH 1/3] x86/boot: fold .bss and .pgtable into .data for flat guest image

The vmxbooter loads the guest as a flat binary: arch/x86/boot/vmlinux.bin is
produced with `objcopy -O binary` and then .incbin'd into kernel.img, which
kmain.c copies to a fixed physical address and enters at 0x900200.

With .bss and .pgtable as separate output sections, the flat binary's layout no
longer matches the load/size arithmetic kmain.c performs (boot_params.hdr.init_size
and the guest copy loop). Folding them into .data keeps the image contiguous and
the offsets predictable.

This is load-bearing for the vmxbooter boot path and has no meaning for a
normally-booted kernel.

---
--- a/arch/x86/boot/compressed/vmlinux.lds.S
+++ b/arch/x86/boot/compressed/vmlinux.lds.S
@@ -48,24 +48,21 @@
 		*(.data.*)
 		*(.bss.efistub)
 		_edata = . ;
-	}
-	. = ALIGN(L1_CACHE_BYTES);
-	.bss : {
+
+		. = ALIGN(L1_CACHE_BYTES);
 		_bss = . ;
 		*(.bss)
 		*(.bss.*)
 		*(COMMON)
 		. = ALIGN(8);	/* For convenience during zeroing */
 		_ebss = .;
-	}
-#ifdef CONFIG_X86_64
-       . = ALIGN(PAGE_SIZE);
-       .pgtable : {
+	
+		. = ALIGN(PAGE_SIZE);
 		_pgtable = . ;
 		*(.pgtable)
 		_epgtable = . ;
 	}
-#endif
+
 	. = ALIGN(PAGE_SIZE);	/* keep ZO size page aligned */
 	_end = .;
 
